---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  PeriodicTable as PeriodicTableContainer,
  ElementalNamesTree as ElementTree,
  ElementalNamesSingleChar as ArrayVisualizer,
  ElementalNamesAllPossibilities,
  ComplexityBenchmark,
  ElementSlotMachine,
  EricaBacktrackVisualizer,
  ElementalNameChecker,
} from "../../components/cave/nombres-elementales";
import RevealCode from "../../components/RevealCode";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="Elemental Names"
  description="Form names using the initials of chemical elements"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Among beakers and test tubes, two chemists are about to welcome their
        babies. Everything is ready. Except the names. They want the names to
        use chemical symbols only. If a name can't be broken down, it doesn't
        pass.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="fragment">
      <PeriodicTableContainer interactive={false} client:load />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p></p>
      <pre>
        - "I like Liam."
        — "Lithium. Americium. Perfect."
      </pre>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ElementSlotMachine
        client:load
        name="Liam"
        tokens={[{ symbol: "Li" }, { symbol: "Am" }]}
        playOn="revealVisible"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p></p>
      <pre>
        - "What about Nora?"
        — "Nitrogen. Oxygen. Radium. That works."
      </pre>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ElementSlotMachine
        client:load
        name="Nora"
        tokens={[{ symbol: "N" }, { symbol: "O" }, { symbol: "Ra" }]}
        playOn="revealVisible"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <pre>
        - "Emma is a nice name too."
        — "Hmm... no. That doesn't work."
      </pre>
      <p>
        They can't keep checking names by hand. They need an automated way to
        spot elemental names.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ElementSlotMachine
        client:load
        name="Emma"
        tokens={[null, null]}
        playOn="revealVisible"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <!-- Cheese Bytes Intro -->
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        My first thought was: this is trivial. We just scan the name from left
        to right. Letter by letter. If it matches an element, we move forward.
        Let's try "Coco".
        <span>Coco</span>
        Carbon, oxygen, carbon, oxygen.
      </p>
      <p>
        That seems to work. Some other names work too.
        <span>Whisky</span>
        So far, everything looks easy. But then we hit a problem. Take the name "Erica".
        If we only take one letter at a time, it fails. There is no element with symbol
        E. No R. No A. So, is "Erica" really not elemental?
      </p>
    </aside>
    <ArrayVisualizer client:load />
  </section>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's look closer. The periodic table does not only have one-letter
        symbols. It also has many two-letter symbols. For example, Erbium. And
        Calcium. With those, "Erica" can be formed.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <PeriodicTableContainer client:load />
  </section>
  <section>
    <div style="height: 14em">
      <ElementalNamesAllPossibilities initialText="Erica" showInput={false} />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        So now we know "Erica" is elemental. But how do we validate this in a
        systematic way? Let's start from the left. "E" does not exist. So we try
        two letters. "Er". That works. We move forward. Now we have "ica". "I"
        works. Iodine. We move forward again. Now we have "ca". "C" works.
        Carbon. We move forward. Now we have "a". "A" does not exist. So this
        fails. If we stop here, we conclude that "Erica" does not work. But that
        conclusion is wrong.
      </p>
      <p>
        The mistake happened earlier. When we matched "C", we had another valid
        option. Instead of taking "C", we could have taken "Ca". Calcium. And
        that would have finished the name. The question is not whether we take
        one or two letters. We need to try both. If we commit too early and
        never go back, we can miss a valid solution.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full">
      <EricaBacktrackVisualizer client:load playOn="revealVisible" />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        We can represent all possibilities as a tree. Each node represents a
        remaining name. From that position, we branch on every valid symbol.
        Some branches fail. Others continue. As soon as one branch reaches the
        end, we know the name is elemental.
      </p>
      <p></p>
    </aside>
    <div style={{ height: "70vh" }}>
      <ElementTree showInput={true} initialText="Erica" client:load />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="code-naive-recursion" class="text-left text-sm">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        We can implement this with a recursive function. We start with a set of
        valid symbols, all in lower case. We also assume the input name is in
        lower case.
      </p>
      <p>
        First, we define the base case. An empty name is elemental. Then we try
        to match one letter. If it matches a symbol, we recursively check the
        rest of the name. If that returns true, we return true. If that fails,
        we try two letters. Again, if it matches a symbol, we recursively check
        the rest. If that returns true, we return true. If neither option works,
        we return false.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="typing"
      code={`#STEP-1
ELEMENTS = {
    'h','he',
    'li','be','b','c','n','o','f','ne',
    'na','mg','al','si','p','s','cl','ar',
    'k','ca','sc','ti','v','cr','mn','fe','co','ni','cu','zn','ga','ge','as','se','br','kr',
    'rb','sr','y','zr','nb','mo','tc','ru','rh','pd','ag','cd','in','sn','sb','te','i','xe',
    'cs','ba','la','ce','pr','nd','pm','sm','eu','gd','tb','dy','ho','er','tm','yb','lu',
    'hf','ta','w','re','os','ir','pt','au','hg','tl','pb','bi','po','at','rn',
    'fr','ra','ac','th','pa','u','np','pu','am','cm','bk','cf','es','fm','md','no','lr',
    'rf','db','sg','bh','hs','mt','ds','rg','cn','fl','lv','ts','og'
}

def is_elemental(name: str) -> bool:  # assume name is lowercase
    # STEP-2
    if name == "":
        return True

    # STEP-3
    if name[:1] in ELEMENTS and is_elemental(name[1:]):
        # STEP-4
        return True

    # STEP-5
    if len(name) >= 2 and name[:2] in ELEMENTS and is_elemental(name[2:]):
        # STEP-6
        return True

    # STEP-7
    return False`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        For many names, this works just fine. Liam is fast. Nora is fast. Emma
        is fast. Even Coco is fast. But how well does this scale? Let's try
        longer inputs. We keep repeating "coco". Again and again. Still fast.
        Almost the same time.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now we add one more letter at the end. Just one "A". Suddenly... it
        explodes. Same code. Same logic. One extra letter. So maybe the "A" is
        the problem? Let's remove one "o". Now it ends in "Ca". Fast again.
        That's strange. What's happening?
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ElementalNameChecker client:visible initialValue="" debounceMs={200} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Some names do not branch much.
        <span>Sophia</span>
        There is only one valid path. "Coco" is different. At every step, there are
        two valid choices. Co. Or C and O. This creates a very wide tree. So why is
        a very long repetition of "Coco" still fast?
      </p>
      <p>
        The algorithm explores one branch at a time. As soon as it finds a valid
        one, it stops. In this case, the first branch succeeds. But when we add
        an "A" at the end, none of them works. Now it has to explore the entire
        tree. That's why it explodes.
      </p>
      <p>
        If instead we end with "Ca", a valid path appears again. The algorithm
        stops early. It's not about how many branches exist. It's about how many
        we must explore before we can stop.
      </p>
      <p>
        At first glance, this looks unavoidable. If the tree grows wide, we must
        explore all possibilities. But not necessarily. If we look closely, many
        branches are actually the same. We are solving the same subproblems
        again and again. We can be smarter.
      </p>
      <p>
        We can save the result for each remaining name. If we reach the same
        position again, we reuse the result. This technique is called
        memoization. With memoization, each position is solved only once. The
        algorithm becomes much faster. Even for names with many possibilities.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div style={{ height: "70vh" }}>
      <ElementTree showInput={true} initialText="Coco" client:load />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="code-final" class="text-left text-sm">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's translate this into code. We create an empty dictionary for
        memoization. At the start of the function, we check if this remaining
        name was already solved. If it was, we return the stored result
        immediately.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then we run the same logic as before. But before returning, we store the
        result in the dictionary. This way, each remaining name is computed only
        once. We avoid repeating the same work.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="typing"
      code={`ELEMENTS = {
    'h','he',
    'li','be','b','c','n','o','f','ne',
    'na','mg','al','si','p','s','cl','ar',
    'k','ca','sc','ti','v','cr','mn','fe','co','ni','cu','zn','ga','ge','as','se','br','kr',
    'rb','sr','y','zr','nb','mo','tc','ru','rh','pd','ag','cd','in','sn','sb','te','i','xe',
    'cs','ba','la','ce','pr','nd','pm','sm','eu','gd','tb','dy','ho','er','tm','yb','lu',
    'hf','ta','w','re','os','ir','pt','au','hg','tl','pb','bi','po','at','rn',
    'fr','ra','ac','th','pa','u','np','pu','am','cm','bk','cf','es','fm','md','no','lr',
    'rf','db','sg','bh','hs','mt','ds','rg','cn','fl','lv','ts','og'
}

# STEP-1
memo: dict[str, bool] = {}

# STEP-0
def is_elemental(name: str) -> bool:  # assume name is lowercase
    # STEP-2
    if name in memo:
        return memo[name]

    # STEP-0
    if name == "":
        return True

    if name[:1] in ELEMENTS and is_elemental(name[1:]):
        # STEP-3
        memo[name] = True
        # STEP-0
        return True

    if len(name) >= 2 and name[:2] in ELEMENTS and is_elemental(name[2:]):
        # STEP-4
        memo[name] = True
        # STEP-0
        return True

    # STEP-5
    memo[name] = False
    # STEP-0
    return False`}
    />
  </section>

  <section style={{ height: "60vh" }}>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        The algorithm becomes much faster. Performance now depends mainly on the
        length of the name, not on the size of the tree.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ElementalNameChecker
        client:visible
        initialValue=""
        debounceMs={200}
        showMemoToggle={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="text-sm">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        And with that, we have solved the problem. There are other ways to
        approach it as well, but this idea is the key. Before we finish, take a
        closer look at the implementation. Some Python-specific details affect
        time complexity. If you figured it out, let me know in the comments.
      </p>
      <p>
        This was today's cheese bytes. Remember, the code matters but the idea
        comes first.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="typing"
      code={`ELEMENTS = {
    'h','he',
    'li','be','b','c','n','o','f','ne',
    'na','mg','al','si','p','s','cl','ar',
    'k','ca','sc','ti','v','cr','mn','fe','co','ni','cu','zn','ga','ge','as','se','br','kr',
    'rb','sr','y','zr','nb','mo','tc','ru','rh','pd','ag','cd','in','sn','sb','te','i','xe',
    'cs','ba','la','ce','pr','nd','pm','sm','eu','gd','tb','dy','ho','er','tm','yb','lu',
    'hf','ta','w','re','os','ir','pt','au','hg','tl','pb','bi','po','at','rn',
    'fr','ra','ac','th','pa','u','np','pu','am','cm','bk','cf','es','fm','md','no','lr',
    'rf','db','sg','bh','hs','mt','ds','rg','cn','fl','lv','ts','og'
  }

memo: dict[str, bool] = {}

def is_elemental(name: str) -> bool:  # assume name is lowercase
    if name in memo:
        return memo[name]

    if name == "":
        return True

    if name[:1] in ELEMENTS and is_elemental(name[1:]):
        memo[name] = True
        return True

    if len(name) >= 2 and name[:2] in ELEMENTS and is_elemental(name[2:]):
        memo[name] = True
        return True

    memo[name] = False
    return False`}
    />
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
